1) Do regular distillation: ft the student on some pose-duplex dataset, where you get the pose-dup to do a forward, along with the teachers, and then train pose against pose mono and text and audio against duplex (KL).
2) Do OPD: get the student to generate rollouts (probably by self-play). Then do KL sampling on the student against the teachers on their respective outputs (ie reverse KL). For each frame freeze all previous frames (no policy gradient)
3) Full credit assignment: do everything that OPD does but now also differentiate through the sampling term: so add a (KL)*grad log(p) into the gradient. This either goes back indefinitely or truncates at some lookback, maybe like 2 seconds. 


can you please explain the difference between these strategies and which is the most sensile for teaching? What are the problems and good things about each? And, please put at the top whether it''s silly to do option 1, because if it''s not I will want to start building that training while I read this answer.','{"type":"disabled"}','never',3796701,0,0,NULL,'4a56a464a44bc4c209ef244f19482737b490c0dd','main','https://huggingface.co/InternalCan/pose-repo','0.165.0','I want to discuss training strategies for a future training run. Here''s what i need to get done: I need to train my pose-duplex model using two teachers, pose monoplex and audio duplex. The pose mono model is able to generate good pose when generating unconditionally. And the audio duplex is competent in speech duplex (audio and conversationally). So we want to get these two to teach the pose-duplex which is neither of these. Three possible ways to do this: 
1) Do regular distillation: ft the student on some pose-duplex dataset, where you get the pose-dup to do a forward, along with the teachers, and then train pose against pose mono and text and audio against duplex (KL).
2) Do OPD: get the student to generate rollouts (probably by self-play). Then do KL sampling on the student against the teachers on their respective outputs (ie reverse KL). For each frame freeze all previous frames (no policy gradient)
3) Full credit assignment: do everything that OPD does but now also differentiate through the sampling term: so add a (KL)*grad log(p) into the gradient. This either goes back indefinitely or truncates at some lookback, maybe like 2 seconds. 


can you please explain the difference between these strategies and which is the most sensile for teaching? What are the problems and good things about each? And, please put at the top whether it''s silly to do option 1, because if it''s not I will want to start building that training while I read this answer.',NULL,NULL,'enabled','gpt-6-astra','ultra',NULL,1793262395166,1793313189187,'user','I want to discuss training strategies for a future training run. Here''s what i need to get done: I need to train my pose-duplex model using two teachers, pose monoplex and audio duplex. The pose mono model is able to generate good pose when generating unconditionally. And the audio duplex is competent in speech duplex (audio and conversationally). So we want to get these two to teach the pose-duplex which is neither of these. Three possible ways to do this: 
1) Do regular distillation: ft the student on some pose-duplex dataset, where you get the pose-dup to do a forward, along with the teachers, and then train pose against pose mono and text and audio against duplex (KL).
2) Do OPD: get the student to generate rollouts (probably by self-play). Then do KL sampling on the student against the teachers on their respective outputs (ie reverse KL). For each frame freeze all previous frames (no policy gradient)
3) Full credit assignment: do everything that OPD does but now also differentiate through the sampling term: so add a (KL)*grad log(p) into the gradient. This either goes back indefinitely or truncates at some lookback, maybe like 2 seconds. 


can you please explain the difference between these strategies and which is the most sensile for teaching? What are the problems and good things about each? And, please put at the top whether it''s silly to do option 1, because if it''s not I will want to start building that training while I read this answer.',1793313173,1793313173113,'paginated','Compare pose-duplex training',0,NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'Codex Desktop',NULL,'user-g8aTlFWBs37cvtMYQhBc15Ok','55231d87-07da-40bf-897d-75ba3d5542b0',NULL);

